#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111678} :activity/id #uuid "f14be6fb-7490-5e50-9e2d-7ccd72daa91c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049310 :topic/id #uuid "eb6655db-68e9-5ef3-bf7a-81223e92dac0" :topic/math-academy-id 1414} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111814 :tutorial/content "Some linear equations have **infinitely many solutions.** \n\nFor example, consider the following equation: \n\n$$\nx+5 = x + 5\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation by subtracting $x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ x+5-x & =x+5-x \\\\ 5 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, what does $x$ equal? The statement $5=5$ is *always* true, irrespective of the value of $x.$ This tells us that *any* value of $x$ is a solution to the equation!\n\nDon't believe it? If we substitute any value, say $x=3,$ into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ 3+5 & =3+5 \\\\ 8 & =8.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's important to note that substituting *any* value for $x$ into the equation will lead to a true statement. For example, the values \n\n$$\nx=42,\\qquad x=-\\dfrac 1 4, \\qquad x=1.2345\n$$\n\nare *all* solutions to the original equation.\n\nTo summarize:\n\n- Our original equation has *infinitely many solutions.*\n\n- The solution of the equation is \"*any (real) value of* $x$\"." :tutorial/id #uuid "edb6ae26-e191-5d7d-9ce8-ad9ee36655f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11389 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "eab36dd4-7684-5fb5-a213-f02f98e0da39" :step/math-academy-id 476188 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110372 :question/id #uuid "6355ac1e-bb4d-5133-9917-04dfb2bc5a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-18854"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476103} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297196}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "310129a9-81a2-54d5-b227-633c2cb1b8d6" :step/math-academy-id 476189 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111680}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111818 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nFor example, each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x=2x,\\,\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{1}{3}x\\,-2.16x=-2.16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo show that the general equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ we can substitute $a$ for $b$ in our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =bx \\\\ ax & =(a)x \\\\ ax & =ax\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =ax \\\\ ax-ax & =ax-ax \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation, $0=0,$ is always true, regardless of the value of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ and any value of $x$ is a solution to the equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "8428afc7-2e9a-55d0-9f13-7a2acdf8fd7d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13429 :tutorial/title "Conditions for an Equation To Have Infinitely Many Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "888ce267-7f48-5074-a1ab-42caf954a38c" :step/math-academy-id 476192 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111681}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111815 :tutorial/content "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2qx = 10x,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. If we're given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{10}_{\\large b} \\ x\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n$$\na = 2q, \\qquad b = 10.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b.$ Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2q = 10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2q & =10 \\\\ \\frac{2q}{2} & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ q & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2qx = 10x$ has infinitely many solutions if $q = 5.$\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by substituting $q = 5$ into our original equation. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2qx & =10x \\\\ 2(5)x & =10x \\\\ 10x & =10x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand simplifying this equation by subtracting $10x$ from both sides yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x & =10x \\\\ 10x-10x & =10x-10x \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is true for all values of $x.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "0d28dc5e-68de-53d0-a57c-9072a89eccbb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11486 :tutorial/title "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "2f94c8cb-7345-56bc-b884-f0ac3f242910" :step/math-academy-id 476193 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111682}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110368 :question/id #uuid "4a8f8deb-2ff1-56d2-888a-3e51eb805bc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-17212"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476197} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476193}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"} :step/id #uuid "1e04eac3-4a93-5d72-9a90-2b1099628885" :step/math-academy-id 476194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111819 :tutorial/content "Each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n2x + 1 = 2x + 1, \\qquad \\dfrac13x -2 = \\dfrac13x -2, \\qquad 1-x = 1 - x\n$$\n\nMore generally, suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d,\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. Then, this equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nNote that if we substitute $c=a$ and $d=b$ into this equation, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =cx+d \\\\ ax+b & =(a)x+(b) \\\\ ax+b & =ax+b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =ax+b \\\\ ax+b-ax & =ax+b-ax \\\\ b & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation, $b = b,$ is always true. Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions, and any real value of $x$ is a solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "7498a4f8-36b5-5d1d-b953-893581bfa9aa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13430 :tutorial/title "Conditions for a General Linear Equation To Have Infinitely Many Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "1d109dfd-fdee-5718-a3d2-a9a96f41ffd0" :step/math-academy-id 476197 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111817 :tutorial/content "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(s - 2)x + t = 6x + 9,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the values of $s$ and $t?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax+b = cx+d{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(s-2)}_{\\large a} \\ x + \\underbrace{t}_{\\large b} = \\underbrace{6}_{\\large c} \\ x + \\underbrace{9}_{\\large d}\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax+b = cx+d$ has infinitely many solutions when $a=c$ and $b=d.$\n\nSo, to determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}})x + {\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}x + {\\color{red}{{9}}}\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we **equate the coefficients** of $x,$ and we **equate the constants:**\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{red}{{9}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $s = 8$ and $t=9,$ our original equation has infinitely many solutions." :tutorial/id #uuid "dc5c5f3e-0731-5ab3-8afd-a2cf24228db1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13403 :tutorial/title "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters in General Linear Equations"} :step/id #uuid "01172b4e-9bb6-512f-9e14-1b2ad76e2a6f" :step/math-academy-id 476198 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110721 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110371 :question/id #uuid "6eeec12a-5da8-5113-916a-58f4ca807207" :question/math-academy-id "e-18852"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477026} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477021}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "c8fdb7d5-63c2-55ac-a293-d9536babfe47" :step/math-academy-id 476199}] :activity/title "Linear Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110810}]}
